rustsocket.library beta
=======================

rustsocket.library is a TCP/IP stack for AmigaOS 4.1 Final Edition that
replaces Roadshow's bsdsocket.library. It is written in Rust on the smoltcp
TCP/IP library and works with any SANA-II network driver.

Nothing else changes when you install it: Roadshow's start-up script,
AddNetInterface, the Network prefs, your interface files, RoadshowControl,
ping, tcpdump, the superserver and your network programs (PPC and 68k)
keep working as before, now on rustsocket. Putting Roadshow's file back
returns everything to Roadshow.

Tested so far: an AmigaOne X5000 (its on-board p50x0_eth.device, and an
Intel I210 card with igb.device, both gigabit Ethernet) and QEMU's
AmigaOne machine (virtionet.device). This is a beta: please report what
works and what does not, on any machine.


1. What you need
----------------
- AmigaOS 4.1 Final Edition with Roadshow installed (it is part of the
  system). rustsocket uses Roadshow's tools and configuration files.
- A working network set-up under Roadshow. Set the network up and test it
  with Roadshow first.


2. Install
----------
In a Shell, in this drawer:

    Copy LIBS:bsdsocket.library LIBS:bsdsocket.library.roadshow
    Copy bsdsocket.library LIBS:
    Protect LIBS:bsdsocket.library +rwed

Reboot. To check that rustsocket is running:

    Version bsdsocket.library FULL

shows "rustsocket" on its second line.


3. Uninstall
------------
    Copy LIBS:bsdsocket.library.roadshow LIBS:bsdsocket.library

and reboot. A stack cannot be swapped while it runs, so changing in
either direction always needs a reboot.


4. Settings
-----------
rustsocket reads Roadshow's settings (RoadshowControl, ENV:Roadshow/...).
Where it differs:

- tcp.sendspace (the send buffer of a new TCP connection) is 64 KB, not
  Roadshow's 32 KB. On gigabit Ethernet this sends at the line rate.
- TCP receive buffers grow and shrink with each connection's speed, up
  to tcp.recvspace.max (default 1 MB; 512 KB on a local network). This is
  rustsocket's own option.
- tcp.sendspace.max is the same for send buffers. Its default, 64 KB,
  means no growth. You can raise it with
      RoadshowControl SET tcp.sendspace.max=1048576
  but this has not been shown to make uploads faster.
- Four options of rustsocket's own set up network cards whose driver
  has the SANA-II extensions (next section): if.txcsum, if.rss.queues,
  if.moderation.rx / if.moderation.tx and if.flowcontrol.

Every option, and the few that rustsocket cannot change (for example
udp.cksum, which is always on), are listed in docs/settings.md in the
source repository.


5. Faster network cards: the SANA-II extensions
-----------------------------------------------
Some drivers offer the unofficial SANA-II extensions: igb.device (Intel
I210, I211, I350 and 82576 cards) does. With such a driver rustsocket
reads and writes many frames at once and lets the card check and fill in
checksums and cut large TCP sends into packets. On an X5000 with an I210
this raised receive from 69 to about 95 MB/s, and sending uses about a
fifth less CPU. There is nothing to set up: rustsocket finds them by
itself. Every other driver works as before.

The debug output shows a line "SANA-II extensions version 1 ..." when an
interface is added whose driver has them.

If you suspect them in a problem, turn off the parts that change what is
sent:

    RoadshowControl SET if.txcsum=0

then remove and add the interface again (RemoveNetInterface and
AddNetInterface). RoadshowControl SET lasts until the next reboot; to
keep it, put 0 in the file ENVARC:Roadshow/if/txcsum. The details are in
docs/sana2-extensions.md in the source repository.


6. Known differences from Roadshow
----------------------------------
- RemoveNetInterface removes any interface that no connection uses.
  Roadshow refuses ("Device busy") once an interface has been configured.
- syslog messages go to the debug output (NetLogViewer shows them), not
  to a log file.
- Not done yet: point-to-point interfaces, BOOTP and link-local address
  allocation, multicast, the IP Filter FTP proxy's address rewriting.
- IP headers leave with ID 0 and the Don't Fragment flag set. This is
  allowed and programs do not see it, but tcpdump shows it.

The full list, call by call and tool by tool, is in roadshow-compat.md in
the source repository.


7. If something goes wrong
--------------------------
rustsocket writes what it does to the debug output, in lines that start
with "rustsocket.library" or "rsnet". After a problem, before rebooting:

    DumpDebugBuffer >RAM:debug.txt

If the network is gone and you cannot get at the machine, uninstall from
another boot (for example, boot without the startup-sequence and copy
LIBS:bsdsocket.library.roadshow back).

Please report:
  - the machine and the network driver (and its version),
  - what you did and what happened,
  - RAM:debug.txt, or the lines from it that start with
    "rustsocket.library" or "rsnet".


